1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,605
This is MATH 239, video 5 b. The topic is combinatorial probability, from Sections 1.1.7 and 1.2 of the notes.

2
00:00:12,055 --> 00:00:16,429
After this video, you can find a probability by counting two sets.

3
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:21,039
You can count the complement when the event is hard to count directly.

4
00:00:21,489 --> 00:00:24,923
You can explain why the model changes the answer.

5
00:00:25,373 --> 00:00:28,872
And you can count poker hands with the AND and OR rules.

6
00:00:29,322 --> 00:00:40,885
We add one assumption to counting: every possible outcome is equally likely. Let omega be the set of all outcomes. An event E is a subset of omega.

7
00:00:41,335 --> 00:00:50,849
Each outcome has probability 1 over the size of omega. So the probability of E is the size of E over the size of omega.

8
00:00:51,299 --> 00:01:00,302
Example 1.16. Choose a random subset of 1 to 8. What is the probability that it has at most 3 elements?

9
00:01:00,752 --> 00:01:11,547
An outcome is a subset of 1 to 8. Each of the 8 numbers is in or out, so omega has 2 to the 8, which is 256, elements.

10
00:01:11,997 --> 00:01:20,210
The bars show how many subsets have each size k, from 0 to 8. All the bars add to 256.

11
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:26,996
The event E is the subsets of size 0, 1, 2 or 3. These are the green bars.

12
00:01:27,446 --> 00:01:32,886
By the OR rule, add 1, 8, 28 and 56. That is 93.

13
00:01:33,336 --> 00:01:40,824
So the probability is 93 over 256, which is about 0.363.

14
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:54,842
Example 1.17. Choose a random list of the six letters a to f. What is the probability that the letters f, a, d appear together, in this order? Pause the video and try it.

15
00:01:55,292 --> 00:02:00,433
In such a list, f a d is one block. Treat the block as one new element.

16
00:02:00,883 --> 00:02:10,974
Then the list is a list of four elements: b, c, e and the block f a d. Each list with the block gives exactly one such list, and back.

17
00:02:11,424 --> 00:02:19,531
There are 4 factorial, which is 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, which is 24, lists of four elements.

18
00:02:19,981 --> 00:02:25,335
There are 6 factorial, which is 720, lists of all six letters.

19
00:02:25,785 --> 00:02:31,609
So the probability is 24 over 720, which is 1 over 30.

20
00:02:32,059 --> 00:02:42,363
Example 1.18. Choose a random multiset of size 2 with t types. What is the probability that both elements have the same type?

21
00:02:42,813 --> 00:02:50,323
By the multiset count from video 2, there are 2 plus t minus 1, choose t minus 1, outcomes.

22
00:02:50,773 --> 00:02:56,085
This equals t plus 1 choose 2, which is t plus 1 times t, over 2.

23
00:02:56,535 --> 00:03:02,636
For t equal to 4, the grid shows the multisets. 5 times 4 over 2 is 10.

24
00:03:03,086 --> 00:03:13,667
The event has t outcomes: choose one type, and take two elements of it. These are the green cells on the diagonal. For t equal to 4, there are 4.

25
00:03:14,117 --> 00:03:21,520
So the probability is t over the quantity t plus 1 times t over 2. That is 2 over t plus 1.

26
00:03:21,970 --> 00:03:30,397
For t equal to 4, it is 4 over 10, which is 0.4. As t grows, the probability goes to 0.

27
00:03:30,847 --> 00:03:41,833
Exercise 1.13 asks about a random permutation of 1 to 7. There are 7 factorial, which is 5040, permutations.

28
00:03:42,283 --> 00:03:48,683
Part 1: what is the probability that 1 and 2 are next to each other? Pause the video and try it.

29
00:03:49,133 --> 00:03:55,256
Use a block again. The block holds 1 and 2 in one of 2 orders: 1 2 or 2 1.

30
00:03:55,706 --> 00:04:03,962
Then we list 6 elements: the block and the other five numbers. There are 6 factorial, which is 720, such lists.

31
00:04:04,412 --> 00:04:12,497
By the AND rule, there are 2 times 720, which is 1440, permutations.

32
00:04:12,947 --> 00:04:20,030
So the probability is 1440 over 5040, which is 2 over 7.

33
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:24,555
Part 2: what is the probability that 1 is to the left of 2?

34
00:04:25,005 --> 00:04:32,941
Swap the numbers 1 and 2 in a permutation. A permutation with 1 on the left goes to one with 2 on the left.

35
00:04:33,391 --> 00:04:38,575
Swap again, and you are back. So the swap is a bijection between the two halves.

36
00:04:39,025 --> 00:04:49,350
So each half has 5040 over 2, which is 2520, permutations. The probability is 1 over 2.

37
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:56,989
Part 3: what is the probability that no two odd numbers are next to each other? Pause the video and try it.

38
00:04:57,439 --> 00:05:03,775
There are four odd numbers, 1, 3, 5 and 7, and three even numbers, 2, 4 and 6.

39
00:05:04,225 --> 00:05:17,473
Four odd numbers with a gap between each pair need at least 4 plus 3, which is 7, places. There are exactly 7 places. So the odd numbers are in places 1, 3, 5 and 7.

40
00:05:17,923 --> 00:05:23,961
Here is one permutation with this pattern: odd, even, odd, even, odd, even, odd.

41
00:05:24,411 --> 00:05:30,277
The odd numbers fill their four places in 4 factorial, which is 24, orders.

42
00:05:30,727 --> 00:05:36,317
The even numbers fill their three places in 3 factorial, which is 6, orders.

43
00:05:36,767 --> 00:05:43,103
By the AND rule, there are 24 times 6, which is 144, permutations.

44
00:05:43,553 --> 00:05:50,422
So the probability is 144 over 5040, which is 1 over 35.

45
00:05:50,872 --> 00:06:06,616
Example 1.20. Let p of n be the probability that at least two of n random people have the same birthday. The notes use 365 days, and assume that birthdays are uniform and independent.

46
00:06:07,066 --> 00:06:15,258
p of 1 is 0. If n is more than 365, p of n is 1, because two people must share a day.

47
00:06:15,708 --> 00:06:24,113
For other n, at least two equal birthdays is hard to count directly. The complement is easy: all n birthdays are different.

48
00:06:24,563 --> 00:06:29,662
List the people P 1 to P n. An outcome is the list of their birthdays.

49
00:06:30,112 --> 00:06:34,379
Try a small case first: a year of 4 days, and 3 people.

50
00:06:34,829 --> 00:06:40,162
Each person has 4 choices, so there are 4 cubed, which is 64, lists.

51
00:06:40,612 --> 00:06:50,959
All birthdays different: the first person has 4 choices, the second 3, and the third 2. 4 times 3 times 2 is 24 lists.

52
00:06:51,409 --> 00:07:01,265
So the probability of a common birthday is 1 minus 24 over 64. That is 40 over 64, which is 5 over 8.

53
00:07:01,715 --> 00:07:18,547
With 365 days, the lists with all birthdays different are the partial lists of length n of the 365 days. There are 365 factorial over 365 minus n factorial of them.

54
00:07:18,997 --> 00:07:23,647
All lists of n birthdays number 365 to the n.

55
00:07:24,097 --> 00:07:28,236
So 1 minus p of n is the ratio of these two counts.

56
00:07:28,686 --> 00:07:31,609
And p of n is 1 minus the ratio.

57
00:07:32,059 --> 00:07:36,837
The blue curve is p of n for n from 1 to 80. It rises fast.

58
00:07:37,287 --> 00:07:48,039
The table gives some values. At n equal to 10, p of n is 0.117. At n equal to 20, it is 0.411.

59
00:07:48,489 --> 00:07:57,577
At n equal to 30, it is already 0.706. At n equal to 50, it is 0.970.

60
00:07:58,027 --> 00:08:08,395
The orange point is n equal to 23. There, p of 23 is 0.507297, just above one half.

61
00:08:08,845 --> 00:08:14,371
So in a group of 23 people, a shared birthday is slightly more likely than not.

62
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:30,245
Example 1.21 shows that a probability depends on the model. A packet of Wine Gums has 10 candies. Each candy has one of five flavours. What is the probability of exactly k purple candies?

63
00:08:30,695 --> 00:08:36,561
In the dice model, we record the order of the candies in the roll, from bottom to top.

64
00:08:37,011 --> 00:08:45,459
Here is a second roll. It has the same candies in another order. In the dice model, these two rolls are different outcomes.

65
00:08:45,909 --> 00:08:58,795
Each candy has 5 choices. So there are 5 to the 10, which is 9,765,625, outcomes. That is nearly 10 million.

66
00:08:59,245 --> 00:09:04,023
In the multiset model, only the number of candies of each flavour matters.

67
00:09:04,473 --> 00:09:11,236
Both rolls give the same multiset: 2 green, 3 yellow, 2 orange, 2 red and 1 purple.

68
00:09:11,686 --> 00:09:24,038
A multiset of size 10 with 5 types: there are 10 plus 5 minus 1, choose 5 minus 1, outcomes. That is 14 choose 4, which is 1001.

69
00:09:24,488 --> 00:09:31,037
Each model makes its own outcomes equally likely. So the two models can give different probabilities.

70
00:09:31,487 --> 00:09:36,074
First the dice model. Fix k, for example k equal to 2.

71
00:09:36,524 --> 00:09:41,537
Choose the positions of the k purple candies. There are 10 choose k ways.

72
00:09:41,987 --> 00:09:48,174
For k equal to 2, 10 choose 2 is 10 times 9 over 2, which is 45.

73
00:09:48,624 --> 00:09:57,072
Each of the other 10 minus k candies has one of the 4 other flavours. So there are 4 to the 10 minus k ways for them.

74
00:09:57,522 --> 00:10:06,503
For k equal to 2, there are 8 other candies: 4 to the 8, which is 65536, ways.

75
00:10:06,953 --> 00:10:12,436
By the AND rule, multiply. Then divide by all 5 to the 10 outcomes.

76
00:10:12,886 --> 00:10:34,006
For k equal to 2: 45 times 65536 is 2,949,120. Divide by 9,765,625. That is 0.301990.

77
00:10:34,456 --> 00:10:40,088
Now the multiset model. Fix k equal to 2 again. Two candies are purple.

78
00:10:40,538 --> 00:10:45,679
The other 10 minus k candies form a multiset with the 4 other flavours.

79
00:10:46,129 --> 00:10:54,641
Their numbers m G, m Y, m O and m R add to 10 minus k. For k equal to 2, they add to 8.

80
00:10:55,091 --> 00:11:05,865
By the multiset count, there are 10 minus k plus 4 minus 1, choose 4 minus 1, such multisets. That is 13 minus k choose 3.

81
00:11:06,315 --> 00:11:18,091
For k equal to 2: 8 plus 4 minus 1 is 11. 11 choose 3 is 11 times 10 times 9 over 6, which is 165.

82
00:11:18,541 --> 00:11:21,655
Divide by all 1001 multisets.

83
00:11:22,105 --> 00:11:31,727
So m of 2 is 165 over 1001, which is 0.164835.

84
00:11:32,177 --> 00:11:38,022
Here are both models side by side. The blue bars are d of k, the dice model.

85
00:11:38,472 --> 00:11:55,240
In the dice model, d of 0 is 0.107, and d of 2 is 0.302, the largest bar. d of 10 is 1 over 9,765,625.

86
00:11:55,690 --> 00:11:59,423
The orange bars are m of k, the multiset model.

87
00:11:59,873 --> 00:12:14,700
In the multiset model, m of 0 is 0.286, the largest bar. m of 2 is 0.165, and m of 10 is 1 over 1001. The bars fall steadily.

88
00:12:15,150 --> 00:12:22,979
So the two models give different answers. Only measurements of real packets can tell which model is closer to the truth.

89
00:12:23,429 --> 00:12:30,277
Why are they different? One multiset comes from many sequences, and the number changes with the multiset.

90
00:12:30,727 --> 00:12:37,959
This number is the multinomial coefficient: n factorial over the product of the m i factorials.

91
00:12:38,409 --> 00:12:56,969
For 2, 3, 2, 2, 1, it is 10 factorial over 2 factorial, 3 factorial, 2 factorial, 2 factorial and 1 factorial. That is 3,628,800 over 48, which is 75600.

92
00:12:57,419 --> 00:13:02,177
A multiset with all 10 candies purple comes from only 1 sequence.

93
00:13:02,627 --> 00:13:12,013
Section 1.2.4 counts poker hands. Each column of the grid is a value, from Ace to King. There are 13 values.

94
00:13:12,463 --> 00:13:19,610
Each row is a suit. There are 4 suits. So the deck has 13 times 4, which is 52, cards.

95
00:13:20,060 --> 00:13:27,057
A hand is any 5 cards. The order does not matter, so a hand is a 5 element subset of the deck.

96
00:13:27,507 --> 00:13:31,646
The orange cells are one hand. It has one pair: two queens.

97
00:13:32,096 --> 00:13:39,349
Practice problem P1-2 asks: how many distinct poker hands are there? Pause the video and try it.

98
00:13:39,799 --> 00:14:00,215
A hand is a 5 element subset of a 52 element set. So there are 52 choose 5 hands. That is 52 times 51 times 50 times 49 times 48, over 5 factorial, which is 2,598,960.

99
00:14:00,665 --> 00:14:06,276
For the hand types, remember one rule: an Ace can be below 2 or above King.

100
00:14:06,726 --> 00:14:15,409
Example 1.22 counts two types. First the Straight: five consecutive values, but not all of the same suit.

101
00:14:15,859 --> 00:14:19,272
The orange hand is 8, 9, 10, Jack, Queen.

102
00:14:19,722 --> 00:14:26,783
Choose the values. The lowest value can be Ace, 2, and so on up to 10. So there are 10 choices.

103
00:14:27,233 --> 00:14:32,673
The last run is 10, Jack, Queen, King, Ace. Here the Ace is above the King.

104
00:14:33,123 --> 00:14:40,355
AND choose a suit for each of the five cards: 4 to the 5, which is 1024, choices.

105
00:14:40,805 --> 00:14:52,155
But 4 of these choices give all five cards one suit. That is a Straight Flush, so remove these 4. 1024 minus 4 is 1020.

106
00:14:52,605 --> 00:14:58,109
So there are 10 times 1020, which is 10200, Straights.

107
00:14:58,559 --> 00:15:05,833
Next, the Busted hand: any hand that is not of a better type. So it has no pair, no straight and no flush.

108
00:15:06,283 --> 00:15:08,651
The orange hand is one example.

109
00:15:09,101 --> 00:15:18,637
No pair means five different values. There are 13 choose 5, which is 1287, sets of five values.

110
00:15:19,087 --> 00:15:31,781
No straight means that the values are not one of the 10 runs. So 1287 minus 10, which is 1277, value sets remain.

111
00:15:32,231 --> 00:15:40,231
No flush means that the suits are not all equal: 4 to the 5 minus 4, which is 1020, choices.

112
00:15:40,681 --> 00:15:52,521
By the AND rule, there are 1277 times 1020, which is 1,302,540, Busted hands.

113
00:15:52,971 --> 00:16:07,456
1,302,540 over 2,598,960 is 0.501. So about half of all hands are Busted.

114
00:16:07,906 --> 00:16:22,903
Exercise 1.14 asks for the other rows of the table. Try two of them: Full House and Two Pair. A Full House has three cards of one value and two cards of another value. Pause the video and try it.

115
00:16:23,353 --> 00:16:27,897
Choose the value of the three cards: 13 ways. Here it is 9.

116
00:16:28,347 --> 00:16:32,721
Choose 3 of its 4 suits: 4 choose 3, which is 4, ways.

117
00:16:33,171 --> 00:16:43,048
Choose the value of the pair from the other 12 values. Here it is Ace. Then choose 2 of its 4 suits: 4 choose 2, which is 6, ways.

118
00:16:43,498 --> 00:16:50,751
By the AND rule, 13 times 4 times 12 times 6 is 3744.

119
00:16:51,201 --> 00:17:02,679
Two Pair: choose the two pair values as a set: 13 choose 2, which is 78, ways. Do not choose them in order, because the two pairs play the same role.

120
00:17:03,129 --> 00:17:08,185
Choose 2 suits for each pair: 6 times 6, which is 36, ways.

121
00:17:08,635 --> 00:17:19,536
The fifth card has one of the 11 other values, in any of 4 suits. So 11 times 4, which is 44, cards are possible. Here it is the 5.

122
00:17:19,986 --> 00:17:27,282
78 times 36 times 44 is 123,552.

123
00:17:27,732 --> 00:17:32,511
Here is the table from the notes. N is the number of hands of each type.

124
00:17:32,961 --> 00:17:35,734
p is N over 52 choose 5.

125
00:17:36,184 --> 00:17:39,363
The green rows are the four types that we counted.

126
00:17:39,813 --> 00:17:49,455
Each hand has exactly one type. So by the OR rule, the rows add to 2,598,960.

127
00:17:49,905 --> 00:18:05,777
The other rows use the same method. One Pair is 13 times 4 choose 2, times 12 choose 3 for the three other values, times 4 cubed for their suits. That is 1,098,240.

128
00:18:06,227 --> 00:18:17,747
Trips is 13 times 4 choose 3, times 12 choose 2 for the two other values, times 4 squared. That is 54912.

129
00:18:18,197 --> 00:18:22,805
A computer list of every hand gives all nine counts of the table.

130
00:18:23,255 --> 00:18:30,914
To recap. With equally likely outcomes, a probability is the size of the event over the size of omega.

131
00:18:31,364 --> 00:18:40,985
A block of letters or numbers becomes one element. The block f a d gives 1 over 30, and 1 and 2 together give 2 over 7.

132
00:18:41,435 --> 00:18:50,033
When the event is hard to count, count the complement, as in the birthday problem. p of 23 is 0.507.

133
00:18:50,483 --> 00:18:54,707
The model decides what an outcome is, as the Wine Gums show.

134
00:18:55,157 --> 00:19:06,869
Poker hands use the AND rule for values and suits, and subtract the cases of a better type. The next video covers the Binomial Theorem and the binomial series.
